// The E2E harness was never typechecked, and three lifecycle trailers said it
// was. `tsconfig.json` includes ONLY `src`, so `npx tsc --noEmit` — the command
// every packet quotes as `tsc✔` — has never once looked at `e2e/`, at
// `playwright.config.ts`, or at the support kit those specs are built on. A
// broken import or a wrong locator type there compiled fine and failed at run
// time, in the one directory that until 2026-08-11 never ran at all. That is
// hard-won law 1 in the tooling: a claim of coverage is not coverage.
//
//   npx tsc -p tsconfig.e2e.json --noEmit
//
// Same strictness as the app. `@types/node` is deliberately NOT listed and NOT
// added as a dependency: the support kit imports `node:fs`/`node:path`/`node:url`
// and reads `process.env`, and Playwright's own bundled types already declare
// enough of both for these files. Adding a dependency to make a typecheck run is
// a bigger change than this lane should make to a shared lockfile — if a future
// file needs fuller Node typings, add `@types/node` in a lane that owns
// package.json and say so here.
//
// `src/vite-env.d.ts` is listed because a spec may import a module out of `src`
// — `CR-016-screen-ids.spec.ts` imports the screen-id map — and that module
// reads `import.meta.env`. Without the declaration file in scope this config
// resolves the module but not the ambient `ImportMeta`, and reports
// `Property 'env' does not exist on type 'ImportMeta'` in a file the app's own
// `tsc` compiles cleanly. One declaration file, not the whole of `src`: the
// point of this config is still that it typechecks `e2e/`.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "include": ["e2e", "playwright.config.ts", "src/vite-env.d.ts"]
}
